[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127274,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Calcifying aponeurotic fibroma (CAF) is a benign, locally infiltrative fibroblastic tumour with chondro-osseous foci, predominantly occurring in the distal extremities of children.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127275,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8816/0 Calcifying aponeurotic fibroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127276,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EE6Y &amp; XH8ZE3 Other specified fibromatous disorders of skin and soft tissue &amp; Calcifying aponeurotic fibroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127277,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> juvenile aponeurotic fibroma; aponeurotic fibroma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127278,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127279,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CAF most commonly involves aponeurotic soft tissue of the palms and feet. Other sites include the thigh, arm, elbow, and knee {9865839}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127280,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CAF typically presents as a solitary painless mass. Multiple lesions are exceptional {9865839}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127281,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CAF is rare. It shows a predilection for male children, although adults can also (more rarely) be affected {9865839}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127282,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology is unknown. Calcifying aponeurotic&ndash;like lesions have been reported in a family with Albright hereditary osteodystrophy {18806481}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127283,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CAFs have a recurrent translocation involving the promoter region of <em>FN1</em> (exon&nbsp;23, 27, or&nbsp;42) on 2q35 and <em>EGF</em> (exon&nbsp;17 or&nbsp;19) on 4q25, resulting in the aberrant overexpression of EGF, which can be detected immunohistochemically {26691015}. This fusion can also be seen in a subset of lipofibromatosis cases, which may represent CAF lacking calcifications {30310176}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127284,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CAF presents as a poorly defined mass measuring &lt;&nbsp;30&nbsp;mm, with a firm consistency and variable calcifications {9865839}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127285,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CAF is infiltrative and composed of bland spindled cells arranged in parallel fascicles with fibrous stroma. Chondroid foci can be seen, with or without calcification, surrounded by epithelioid cells {9865839; 13032926}. Chondroid cells, multinucleated giant cells, and calcific debris may be seen {11335964}.</p>\r\n<h6>Immunohistochemistry</h6>\r\n<p>There is variable expression of MSA, SMA, CD99, S100, and CD68. Rare cases also label for CD34, CD57, PR, and EMA. &beta;-catenin is negative {18939887}.</p>\r\n<h6>Differential diagnosis</h6>\r\n<p>The differential diagnosis includes infantile digital fibromatosis (inclusion body fibromatosis), fibromatosis, and lipofibromatosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127286,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 775,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 833,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 776,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127287,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 776,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Detection of <em>FN1</em>::<em>EGF</em> fusion may be helpful in challenging cases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 836,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 779,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127288,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 779,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> an infiltrative bland spindle cell proliferation in parallel fascicles and fibrous background (fibromatosis-like); epithelioid/chondroid cells with or without calcification.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> <em>FN1</em>::<em>EGF</em> fusion in challenging cases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 834,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 777,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127289,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 777,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 835,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 778,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127290,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 381,
        "headingId": 778,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CAF is benign, but the rate of local recurrence may be as high as 50%, and recurrence may occur years after initial excision. Children aged &lt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;years are at higher risk of local recurrence {9865839}. Rare metastasis and malignant (fibrosarcomatous) transformation have been reported {12377925; 11315790; 3760506}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:36:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]